## Feature #7 Increment 3 (spec §4.7) — restoring the real clock. Virtualization
## is scoped: after restore, time is real again — a real `Async.sleep` reverts to
## the OS timer (real wait) and a real `Instant` measures real elapsed.
##
## v1 RESTORE SURFACE: `TestClock.uninstall` (explicit). The install GUARD's
## `def drop` is the intended RAII restore, but a `TestClock.install` result
## local is tainted out of drop-elaboration today (the Scope/Queue/Stream
## drop-rooting limit), so its drop doesn't fire automatically — a test calls
## `TestClock.uninstall` explicitly (the `Scope.cancel_remaining` precedent;
## `def drop` stays a belt-and-braces net for when drop-rooting is fixed).
##
## Under the virtual clock, a 60s sleep fires instantly via advance. Then
## `uninstall` restores the real clock. A real `Async.sleep(20ms)` through the
## real reactor + an `Instant` measures real elapsed in the 10-500ms window —
## proving restore (a still-virtual clock would hang the real sleep / read
## virtual ~0 elapsed).

use std.time.Instant
use std.time.Duration

def main
  puts "guard start"

  var guard = TestClock.install
  var cx = Context.executor
  var s = Async.sleep(Duration.from_secs(60))
  let _p1 = (&var s).poll(&var cx)
  TestClock.advance(Duration.from_secs(60))
  var fired = 0
  match (&var s).poll(&var cx)
    Poll.Ready(_) -> fired = 1
    Poll.Pending  -> fired = 0
  end
  puts "virtual_fired=#{fired}"

  # Restore the real clock (explicit v1 teardown).
  TestClock.uninstall

  # Real clock now. A real 20ms sleep through the real reactor; Instant measures
  # real elapsed (not virtual).
  let start = Instant.now()
  block_on(Async.sleep(Duration.from_millis(20)))
  let ms = start.elapsed().as_millis()
  if ms >= 10 && ms <= 500
    puts "real_clock_ok"
  else
    puts "real_clock_BAD ms=#{ms}"
  end
  puts "guard done"
end
